    "points": 5,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "One digit was erased in a two-digit number. As a result, the number is $n$ times smaller. Which of the following cannot be $n$?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "3"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "4"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "5"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "6"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "7"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Which digit is erased",
        "body": "Let the number be $10x + y$ with $x \\ge 1$. If the units digit is erased, $10x + y = n x$ needs $n \\ge 10$. So the tens digit is erased and $10x + y = n y$, i.e. $10x = (n - 1)y$.\n\n- $n = 3$: $10x = 2y$, $y = 5x$: $15 \\to 5$ works.\n- $n = 4$: $10x = 3y$, so $y$ is a multiple of 10, i.e. $y = 0$ and then $x = 0$. Impossible.\n- $n = 5$: $10x = 4y$: $25 \\to 5$ works.\n- $n = 6$: $10x = 5y$: $12 \\to 2$ works.\n- $n = 7$: $10x = 6y$: $35 \\to 5$ works.\n\nSo $n$ cannot be 4.\n\n**Answer: (B) 4**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Examples and a contradiction",
        "body": "Examples: $15 = 3 \\cdot 5$, $25 = 5 \\cdot 5$, $12 = 6 \\cdot 2$, $35 = 7 \\cdot 5$, so 3, 5, 6, 7 are possible. For $n = 4$, the remaining digit $d$ satisfies $4d = $ the original number. Erasing the tens digit means the original ends in $d$, but $4d$ ends in $d$ only if $3d$ is a multiple of 10, i.e. $d = 0$, which is not allowed. Erasing the units digit leaves the tens digit $d$, and the original number is at least $10d > 4d$. So $n = 4$ is impossible.\n\n**Answer: (B) 4**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "number-theory",
      "logic"
    ],
    "difficulty": 3,
