    "grade": "9-10",
    "points": 5,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "One of two lines on the coordinate plane has an equation $y = ax + b$ for some real $a$ and $b$. Which of the following equations can the other line have?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2021-g0910/q30.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$y = ax - b$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$y = bx + a$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$y = \\frac{b}{a}x + b$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$y = -bx + a$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$y = \\frac{a}{b}x + a$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "E",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Read the picture",
        "body": "Both lines rise, so both slopes are positive, and both cross the $y$-axis above the origin, so both $y$-intercepts are positive. In particular $a > 0$ and $b > 0$. The steeper line has the larger $y$-intercept, and the lines have different slopes and different intercepts.\n\n- (A) has intercept $-b < 0$; (D) has slope $-b < 0$. Impossible.\n- (C) has the same intercept $b$ as the first line. Impossible.\n- (B) has slope $b$ and intercept $a$. If $a > b$, the line $y = ax + b$ is steeper but has the smaller intercept; if $a < b$ the line $y = bx + a$ is steeper with the smaller intercept. Either way it contradicts the picture.\n- (E) works: for example $a = 2$, $b = 3$ gives $y = 2x + 3$ and $y = \\frac23 x + 2$. The first is steeper and has the larger intercept, the lines meet at $x = -\\frac34$ (left of the $y$-axis), and their $x$-intercepts $-1.5$ and $-3$ are both negative, with the flatter line crossing farther left, as in the picture.\n\n**Answer: (E) $y = \\frac{a}{b}x + a$**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
